Implement ucontext_get_pc / ucontext_set_pc for the Zero VM on Darwin/arm64.

Zero leaves both as ShouldNotCall() stubs. But HotSpot's crash/signal handler
calls os::Posix::ucontext_get_pc the instant ANY signal reaches it, so on iOS
(bsd-aarch64) the stub aborts the whole process:

    Internal Error (os_bsd_zero.cpp:91), Error: ShouldNotCall()
    Current thread is native thread; VM state: not at safepoint (normal execution)

Returning the real program counter from the Darwin/arm64 machine context lets
signal handling proceed normally instead of aborting. Applied in CI
(build-ios-jvm.yml) against openjdk/mobile @ c1ed06a before configure/make.

--- a/src/hotspot/os_cpu/bsd_zero/os_bsd_zero.cpp
+++ b/src/hotspot/os_cpu/bsd_zero/os_bsd_zero.cpp
@@ -90,8 +90,20 @@
 address os::Posix::ucontext_get_pc(const ucontext_t* uc) {
-  ShouldNotCallThis();
-  return nullptr;
+#if defined(__APPLE__) && defined(__aarch64__)
+  // Zero normally never needs the PC, but HotSpot's crash/signal handler calls
+  // this as soon as any signal arrives; on iOS the ShouldNotCall() stub then
+  // aborts the whole process (os_bsd_zero.cpp:91). Hand back the real PC from
+  // the Darwin/arm64 machine context so signal handling can continue.
+  return (address) uc->uc_mcontext->__ss.__pc;
+#else
+  ShouldNotCallThis();
+  return nullptr;
+#endif
 }

 void os::Posix::ucontext_set_pc(ucontext_t * uc, address pc) {
-  ShouldNotCallThis();
+#if defined(__APPLE__) && defined(__aarch64__)
+  uc->uc_mcontext->__ss.__pc = (uint64_t) pc;
+#else
+  ShouldNotCallThis();
+#endif
 }
